            <ArticleTitle>Groin pain beyond the hip: how anatomy predisposes to injury as visualized by musculoskeletal ultrasound and MRI.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1190-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2214/AJR.10.4890</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The purpose of this article is to show ultrasound and MRI examples of the normal anatomic structures and their resulting modifications from trauma and disease.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Although groin pain from hip pathology is well recognized, lower anterior abdominal wall and anterior pelvis structures can be interrelated sources of pain.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
